# Map notes, English source. slug: (neighbourhoods, water, streets). No dashes as punctuation.
T = {
'amsterdam': (
 'The Jordaan packs tiny streets between the western canals, De Pijp is a tight grid south of the centre, and Oud-West and Oost spread out in long nineteenth century blocks. Amsterdam Noord sits on its own across the IJ.',
 'Four main canals, the Herengracht, Keizersgracht, Prinsengracht and Singel, ring the old town. The Amstel runs in from the south, and the wide IJ separates the centre from Noord with ferries crossing day and night.',
 'Inside the ring the streets follow the water in concentric arcs, crossed by short radial streets. Further out the twentieth century plans take over: straight avenues in Oud-Zuid and the garden city layouts of Nieuw-West.'),
'rotterdam': (
 'The modern centre around the Coolsingel was rebuilt after 1940. Kralingen and Hillegersberg are green and leafy, Delfshaven keeps its old harbour houses, and Kop van Zuid rises with towers on the south bank.',
 'The Nieuwe Maas splits the city in two and is crossed by the Erasmusbrug and Willemsbrug. Old harbours like the Oude Haven and Wijnhaven cut into the north bank, and the Kralingse Plas lies to the east.',
 'Wide boulevards and big blocks in the rebuilt centre contrast with the narrow streets of Delfshaven. To the west the docks of the port stretch out in long, straight basins towards the sea.'),
'the-hague': (
 'The historic centre surrounds the Binnenhof, Statenkwartier and Duinoord are elegant and green, Scheveningen faces the sea, and the Zeeheldenkwartier and Archipelbuurt fill the space between the town and the dunes.',
 'The Hofvijver lies next to the parliament, narrow canals still wind through the old centre, and the North Sea coast at Scheveningen with its harbour and pier forms the western edge of the map.',
 'Long avenues lead from the centre to the sea, lined with large villas and woods such as the Haagse Bos and Scheveningse Bosjes. The neighbourhoods follow a calm, orderly grid between them.'),
'utrecht': (
 'The medieval centre gathers around the Dom tower. Wittevrouwen and Lombok are busy nineteenth century districts, Oog in Al is quiet and green, and Leidsche Rijn grows to the west as a new town.',
 'The Oudegracht with its unique wharf cellars runs right through the centre, the moat of the Singel wraps around it, and the Vaartsche Rijn and Merwede canal lead south out of the city.',
 'Inside the Singel the streets are narrow and irregular, following medieval lines. Beyond it the districts open up into neat blocks, crossed by the long Amsterdamsestraatweg heading north.'),
'antwerp': (
 'The old town clusters around the cathedral and the Grote Markt. Het Zuid has wide avenues and museums, Zurenborg is famous for its Art Nouveau houses, and Eilandje rises from the former docks.',
 'The Scheldt bends wide along the west side of the city, with Linkeroever on the far bank. North of the centre the old harbour basins of Eilandje lead into the vast port.',
 'The Leien, a chain of wide boulevards, follow the line of the old city walls and enclose a dense knot of medieval streets. The ring road further out follows the later fortifications.'),
'ghent': (
 'The historic heart lies between the Gravensteen and Saint Bavo, the Patershol is a maze of small lanes, and the student quarter and the Citadelpark fill the south. Dampoort and Sint-Amandsberg lie to the east.',
 'The Leie and the Scheldt meet in the centre, with the Graslei and Korenlei facing each other across the water. Canals such as the Coupure and the Lieve thread through the city.',
 'The medieval centre is a dense tangle of short streets that bend with the rivers. The R40 ring road holds it together, with straighter nineteenth century streets beyond it.'),
'brussels': (
 'The pentagon of the old town holds the Grand Place and the Sablon. Ixelles and Saint-Gilles are lively and dense, the European Quarter lies to the east, and Uccle and the Bois de la Cambre are green and quiet.',
 'The Senne was covered over long ago, so the main water is the Brussels Charleroi canal running through the west. Ponds dot the Bois de la Cambre and the parks, and the forest of Soignes lies to the south.',
 'The small ring follows the shape of the old walls as a pentagon. Grand avenues like the Avenue Louise and the Rue de la Loi lead out of it in straight lines towards the parks.'),
'bruges': (
 'The whole historic centre fits inside an oval ring. The Markt and the Burg are at its heart, Sint-Anna is calm and residential, and the Begijnhof and the Minnewater close the south side.',
 'Canals, the reien, wind through the old town in loops, and a ring canal follows the edge of the former walls. To the north a canal leads straight towards the coast at Zeebrugge.',
 'The streets curve gently between the canals, forming a dense medieval web. The ring road around the centre makes the clear oval shape that stands out on every Bruges map.'),
'berlin': (
 'Mitte holds the historic core around Unter den Linden. Prenzlauer Berg and Kreuzberg are dense Gründerzeit districts, Charlottenburg is grand and western, and Friedrichshain and Neukölln stretch out to the east and south.',
 'The Spree runs from east to west through the centre, splitting around Museum Island. The Landwehrkanal cuts through Kreuzberg, and to the west and south lakes like the Wannsee and the Müggelsee frame the city.',
 'Long, straight avenues and large courtyard blocks define the inner districts. The Tiergarten forms a big green rectangle in the middle, and the former airfield of Tempelhof leaves an open space in the south.'),
'hamburg': (
 'The Altstadt and the Neustadt surround the Rathaus. St. Pauli and Altona sit along the river to the west, Eimsbüttel and Winterhude are leafy and residential, and the HafenCity grows on the old docks.',
 'The Elbe and its harbour dominate the south, while the Alster forms two lakes, the Binnenalster and the Außenalster, in the heart of the city. Canals called Fleete run between the old warehouses.',
 'The streets around the Alster radiate outward like spokes. Around the harbour the grid becomes long and industrial, and the brick rows of the Speicherstadt follow the shape of the canals.'),
'munich': (
 'The Altstadt lies around the Marienplatz. Schwabing and Maxvorstadt are the student and art districts, Haidhausen sits across the river, and Nymphenburg in the west centres on its palace and park.',
 'The Isar runs north to south through the city with wide gravel banks. The Eisbach and other channels flow through the Englischer Garten, and the canal of Nymphenburg leads straight to the palace.',
 'A ring of boulevards marks the old fortifications around the Altstadt. Beyond it Munich spreads out in broad, orderly avenues such as the Leopoldstraße and the Ludwigstraße heading north.'),
'cologne': (
 'The Altstadt surrounds the cathedral on the left bank. The Belgisches Viertel and Ehrenfeld are lively, Südstadt is full of old houses, and Deutz looks back across the river at the skyline.',
 'The Rhine curves through the city and is crossed by famous bridges such as the Hohenzollernbrücke. The Rheinauhafen with its crane houses lies on the left bank south of the centre.',
 'Cologne grew in rings. The semicircle of the Ringe follows the medieval walls, and the green belts further out follow the later forts, giving the map its striking half circle shape on the Rhine.'),
'paris': (
 'The twenty arrondissements spiral outwards from the centre like a snail shell. The Marais, Saint-Germain, Montmartre and the Latin Quarter each have their own pattern, and the Bois de Boulogne and Vincennes frame the city.',
 'The Seine crosses Paris in a wide arc from east to west, around the Île de la Cité and the Île Saint-Louis. The Canal Saint-Martin runs north from the Bastille through the tenth arrondissement.',
 'Haussmann cut long, straight boulevards through the old city, meeting in star shaped squares like the Étoile. Between them the older streets remain narrow and winding, especially on the hill of Montmartre.'),
'nice': (
 'Vieux Nice is a tangle of narrow lanes below the castle hill. Le Port lies to the east, the Carré d’Or and Jean Médecin hold the modern centre, and Cimiez climbs the hills to the north.',
 'The Baie des Anges curves along the whole south of the map. The Paillon river was covered and turned into the Promenade du Paillon, a green strip between the old and new town.',
 'The Promenade des Anglais follows the coast in one long line. Behind it the nineteenth century streets form a regular grid, which breaks into winding roads as they climb the hills.'),
'london': (
 'The City and Westminster form the historic heart. Soho and Covent Garden are dense, Notting Hill and Chelsea are elegant, and Shoreditch, Hackney and Brixton spread out to the east and south.',
 'The Thames winds through London in big loops, most clearly around the Isle of Dogs. Regent’s Canal curves across the north, and parks with lakes such as the Serpentine add water to the centre.',
 'London never had a grid. Its streets grew from villages, so the map is a web of curving roads, crescents and garden squares, with the large green shapes of Hyde Park and Regent’s Park in between.'),
'vienna': (
 'The Innere Stadt is the first district, wrapped by the Ringstraße. Neubau and Josefstadt are lively and cultured, Leopoldstadt lies across the canal, and Döbling climbs into the vineyards in the north.',
 'The Danube runs along the east side with the long Danube Island in the middle. The Danube Canal flows close to the centre, and the Old Danube forms a calm lake beside the river.',
 'The Ringstraße circles the old town where the walls once stood. Outside it the districts radiate out in wedges, and the Gürtel forms a second, wider ring around the inner city.'),
'prague': (
 'Staré Město and Josefov form the old town on the right bank. Malá Strana and the castle district lie across the river, Nové Město holds Wenceslas Square, and Vinohrady and Žižkov rise to the east.',
 'The Vltava curves through the city from south to north, crossed by the Charles Bridge and many others. Islands such as Kampa and Střelecký ostrov sit in the river near the centre.',
 'The old town is a dense web of crooked lanes around squares. Nové Město, planned in the fourteenth century, has wider straight streets, and Vinohrady is a calm late nineteenth century grid.'),
'rome': (
 'The Centro Storico fills the bend of the river. Trastevere lies on the other bank, Monti sits between the Forum and the station, and Prati and the Vatican lie to the north west.',
 'The Tiber winds through Rome in a big S curve, with the Tiber Island in the middle. Fountains and aqueducts carry water throughout the city, but the river is the only line you see on the map.',
 'Ancient roads like the Via Appia and the Via del Corso still shape the city. Narrow alleys open into piazzas, and the seven hills break the pattern of streets into curves and slopes.'),
'venice': (
 'The six sestieri divide the city: San Marco, Castello, Cannaregio, Dorsoduro, San Polo and Santa Croce. The Giudecca lies across the water to the south, and the Lido protects the lagoon from the sea.',
 'The Grand Canal snakes through the city in a reversed S, with around one hundred and fifty smaller canals branching off. The lagoon surrounds everything, with islands like Murano and Burano to the north.',
 'There are no roads, only calli, narrow lanes that twist between buildings and end at the water. On the map Venice looks like a fish, cut into hundreds of tiny islands by the canals.'),
'barcelona': (
 'The Gothic Quarter and El Born form the old town. The Eixample spreads out in its famous grid, Gràcia keeps a village character, and Barceloneta sits on a triangle of land by the sea.',
 'The Mediterranean forms the whole south eastern edge, with long beaches and the Port Vell. The city lies between two rivers, the Llobregat and the Besòs, and the hill of Montjuïc rises by the sea.',
 'Cerdà’s Eixample is a perfect grid of square blocks with cut corners. Diagonal avenues such as the Avinguda Diagonal cross it, while the old town stays a tangle of narrow medieval lanes.'),
'madrid': (
 'The historic centre runs from the Royal Palace to the Puerta del Sol. Malasaña and Chueca are lively, Salamanca is elegant with straight streets, and Lavapiés and La Latina climb south.',
 'The Manzanares river runs along the west, lined by the green Madrid Río park. The lake of the Retiro park and the Casa de Campo lake add water to a mostly dry inland city.',
 'The old town is a knot of narrow streets around plazas. The nineteenth century extension of Salamanca forms a neat grid, and the Paseo de la Castellana cuts a long straight axis north.'),
'lisbon': (
 'Alfama climbs the hill below the castle, Baixa sits in the valley, and Bairro Alto and Chiado sit above it. Belém lies to the west along the river, and Parque das Nações to the east.',
 'Lisbon faces the wide Tagus estuary along its whole south side, crossed by the 25 de Abril bridge and the long Vasco da Gama bridge. The river almost looks like a sea on the map.',
 'The Baixa was rebuilt after the 1755 earthquake as a strict grid. All around it the old districts twist up and down seven hills in steep lanes and stairways.'),
'copenhagen': (
 'Indre By holds the old town around Strøget. Nørrebro and Vesterbro are dense and lively, Østerbro is calm and green, and Christianshavn and Christiania sit across the harbour.',
 'The harbour runs through the centre, with Nyhavn cutting into the old town. The canals of Christianshavn, the city lakes in a row to the west and the Øresund to the east frame the map.',
 'The medieval streets form a compact old town. Beyond the lakes the districts fan out along long straight roads such as Nørrebrogade, with the star shaped Kastellet in the north.'),
'new-york': (
 'Manhattan holds the classic districts from the Financial District to Harlem, with Central Park in the middle. Brooklyn, Queens, the Bronx and Staten Island fill the rest of the five boroughs.',
 'Water surrounds almost everything: the Hudson to the west, the East River, New York Harbor with the Statue of Liberty and the Atlantic coast of Brooklyn and Queens.',
 'Above Houston Street Manhattan follows the 1811 grid of numbered streets and avenues, crossed by Broadway at an angle. Lower Manhattan and Greenwich Village keep their older irregular streets.'),
'los-angeles': (
 'Downtown, Hollywood, Santa Monica, Venice and Silver Lake are only a few of the many districts. Beverly Hills and West Hollywood form their own cities in the middle, and the Valley lies over the hills.',
 'The Pacific coast forms the western edge from Malibu to Long Beach. The concrete Los Angeles River crosses the city, and Marina del Rey forms a large artificial harbour of its own.',
 'Long straight boulevards like Sunset, Wilshire and Santa Monica run for miles. The Santa Monica Mountains break the grid, and freeways cut wide curves through the city.'),
'san-francisco': (
 'The Mission, Haight-Ashbury, North Beach, Chinatown and the Castro each have their own character. The Presidio and Golden Gate Park form large green areas in the west.',
 'The Pacific lies to the west and the bay to the north and east. The Golden Gate Bridge links the city to Marin, and the Bay Bridge leads across to Oakland.',
 'A strict grid of streets runs straight over the steep hills, which is why the streets are so famous. Market Street cuts diagonally through the grid from the Ferry Building to Twin Peaks.'),
'las-vegas': (
 'The Strip with its famous casino resorts runs south of downtown. Fremont Street is the old centre, and suburbs like Summerlin and Henderson spread out into the desert valley.',
 'Water is rare in the Mojave Desert. Lakes in the resorts, the Bellagio fountains and Lake Las Vegas stand out on the map, with Lake Mead a short drive to the east.',
 'Las Vegas is built on a large grid of arterial roads a mile apart. The Strip runs diagonally in one long line, and curving suburban streets fill the blocks in between.'),
'edinburgh': (
 'The Old Town follows the Royal Mile from the castle to Holyrood. The New Town is Georgian and orderly, Leith is the old port, and Stockbridge and Bruntsfield are leafy villages within the city.',
 'The Firth of Forth forms the northern edge, with the harbour of Leith on the shore. The Water of Leith winds through the city, and the Union Canal runs in from the west.',
 'The Old Town is a medieval spine with narrow closes branching off. The New Town is a planned grid of crescents and squares, and Arthur’s Seat adds a hill right next to the centre.'),
'oslo': (
 'Sentrum lies around Karl Johans gate. Grünerløkka is lively along the river, Frogner is elegant, Gamle Oslo is the oldest part, and new waterfront districts like Bjørvika and Tjuvholmen line the fjord.',
 'The Oslofjord forms the southern edge with islands scattered across it. The river Akerselva flows from north to south through the city, and the Holmenkollen hills rise to the north.',
 'The centre has a compact grid of streets planned in the seventeenth century. Around it the districts follow the hills, with forest and lakes close to the edge of the map.'),
'stockholm': (
 'Gamla Stan is the medieval old town on its own island. Södermalm is lively to the south, Östermalm is elegant, Vasastan is residential, and Djurgården is a green park island.',
 'Stockholm is built on fourteen islands where Lake Mälaren meets the Baltic Sea. Bridges and ferries link the islands, and water covers about a third of the city.',
 'Gamla Stan is a maze of narrow medieval lanes. On Östermalm and Vasastan the streets form wide, straight grids, giving the city its mix of island shapes and orderly blocks.'),
}
